    INSERT INTO mcqs (topic_id, question, options, correct_index, explanation) VALUES
    (v_topic_id, 'A 68-year-old male named Earl is seen in the clinic. You suspect physical abuse because of suspicious back bruises that resemble handprints. The patient denies any abuse, stating, ''I just bumped into things.'' The caregiver is cooperative and polite. The clinician is unsure if there is enough evidence to prove abuse. What is the legal standard for reporting in this situation?', '["A report should only be filed if the caregiver confesses to the abuse or if there are eyewitnesses to the physical trauma", "The clinician should wait until the next scheduled visit in 6 months to see if new bruises appear before reporting", "The clinician must conduct a private investigation at the patient''s home to gather definitive proof before filing any report", "The clinician is legally prohibited from reporting unless the patient explicitly consents or admits to being abused", "The clinician must file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) immediately, as the legal standard for reporting is a ''reasonable suspicion'' of abuse or neglect, not absolute proof"]'::jsonb, 4, 'Under mandatory reporting laws in the United States, healthcare professionals are required to report elder abuse, neglect, or exploitation when they have **reasonable suspicion** or **reason to believe** that an older adult is being harmed. **Clinicians do not need to prove that abuse has occurred, nor do they need the patient''s consent to file the report.** The agency (Adult Protective Services) is responsible for investigating and establishing proof. Failure to report suspected abuse can lead to civil or criminal penalties for the clinician. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; First Aid Step 2 CK; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
