    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Hazel is suspected of being neglected by her niece in her own home. A colleague asks you about the difference in reporting pathways for domestic elder abuse versus abuse occurring in an institutional setting (like a nursing home). Which of the following is the correct distinction?', '["The Long-Term Care Ombudsman investigates domestic abuse cases, while APS only investigates nursing home violations", "Institutional abuse is reported to APS, whereas domestic abuse is handled solely by the local ethics committee", "There is no legal reporting mechanism for institutional settings; only private lawsuits can address nursing home neglect", "All elder abuse, regardless of setting, is reported exclusively to the local police department, and clinicians should never contact civil agencies", "Domestic abuse is reported to Adult Protective Services (APS), whereas institutional abuse is reported to the Long-Term Care Ombudsman and the state department of health or regulatory agency"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The reporting pathway for elder abuse depends on the setting: (1) **Domestic setting (home)**: Suspected abuse, neglect, or financial exploitation is reported to **Adult Protective Services (APS)**. (2) **Institutional setting (nursing home, assisted living)**: Suspected abuse is reported to the **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** and/or the **state department of health/licensing agency** that regulates long-term care facilities. Both pathways are legally mandated. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
