    (v_topic_id, 'A 66-year-old male named Charles is suspected of being neglected by his grandson in his own home. A colleague asks you about the difference in reporting pathways for domestic elder abuse versus abuse occurring in an institutional setting (like a nursing home). Which of the following is the correct distinction?', '["The Long-Term Care Ombudsman investigates domestic abuse cases, while APS only investigates nursing home violations", "There is no legal reporting mechanism for institutional settings; only private lawsuits can address nursing home neglect", "Institutional abuse is reported to APS, whereas domestic abuse is handled solely by the local ethics committee", "All elder abuse, regardless of setting, is reported exclusively to the local police department, and clinicians should never contact civil agencies", "Domestic abuse is reported to Adult Protective Services (APS), whereas institutional abuse is reported to the Long-Term Care Ombudsman and the state department of health or regulatory agency"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The reporting pathway for elder abuse depends on the setting: (1) **Domestic setting (home)**: Suspected abuse, neglect, or financial exploitation is reported to **Adult Protective Services (APS)**. (2) **Institutional setting (nursing home, assisted living)**: Suspected abuse is reported to the **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** and/or the **state department of health/licensing agency** that regulates long-term care facilities. Both pathways are legally mandated. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old male named Edward is suspected of being neglected by his grandson in his own home. A colleague asks you about the difference in reporting pathways for domestic elder abuse versus abuse occurring in an institutional setting (like a nursing home). Which of the following is the correct distinction?', '["Domestic abuse is reported to Adult Protective Services (APS), whereas institutional abuse is reported to the Long-Term Care Ombudsman and the state department of health or regulatory agency", "There is no legal reporting mechanism for institutional settings; only private lawsuits can address nursing home neglect", "Institutional abuse is reported to APS, whereas domestic abuse is handled solely by the local ethics committee", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman investigates domestic abuse cases, while APS only investigates nursing home violations", "All elder abuse, regardless of setting, is reported exclusively to the local police department, and clinicians should never contact civil agencies"]'::jsonb, 0, 'The reporting pathway for elder abuse depends on the setting: (1) **Domestic setting (home)**: Suspected abuse, neglect, or financial exploitation is reported to **Adult Protective Services (APS)**. (2) **Institutional setting (nursing home, assisted living)**: Suspected abuse is reported to the **Long-Term Care Ombudsman** and/or the **state department of health/licensing agency** that regulates long-term care facilities. Both pathways are legally mandated. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old male named Earl is evaluated in the clinic. During a private interview, Earl admits that his adult son frequently hits him and takes his pension checks. A formal cognitive assessment confirms that Earl has intact decision-making capacity. Earl begs you not to report the son, stating, ''He is all I have, and I don''t want him to go to jail. I refuse any help from social services.'' What is the most appropriate ethical and legal action for the clinician?', '["Respect the patient''s autonomy and do not file a report, as a competent patient''s refusal overrides the physician''s mandatory reporting duty", "Immediately notify local law enforcement to have the son arrested, bypassing APS and ignoring the patient''s statements", "File a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS) as required by state law, document the injuries and conversation meticulously, and work with the patient on a safety and harm-reduction plan", "Confront the son in the waiting room and threaten him with legal action, but do not document the encounter in the medical record", "Involuntarily admit the patient to a psychiatric facility for ''pathological codependency'' to separate him from the son"]'::jsonb, 2, 'In the majority of US states, healthcare professionals are **mandated reporters** for elder abuse and must file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) upon **reasonable suspicion**, **regardless of the patient''s consent or cognitive capacity**. While the patient''s autonomy must be respected regarding what services they accept from APS (a competent patient can refuse shelter or legal action), the clinician *must* fulfill their legal obligation to report. The clinician should support the patient, document all findings objectively, and discuss safety planning. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Eleanor is evaluated in the clinic. During a private interview, Eleanor admits that her adult son frequently hits her and takes her pension checks. A formal cognitive assessment confirms that Eleanor has intact decision-making capacity. Eleanor begs you not to report the son, stating, ''He is all I have, and I don''t want him to go to jail. I refuse any help from social services.'' What is the most appropriate ethical and legal action for the clinician?', '["Involuntarily admit the patient to a psychiatric facility for ''pathological codependency'' to separate her from the son", "File a mandatory report with Adult Protective Services (APS) as required by state law, document the injuries and conversation meticulously, and work with the patient on a safety and harm-reduction plan", "Confront the son in the waiting room and threaten him with legal action, but do not document the encounter in the medical record", "Immediately notify local law enforcement to have the son arrested, bypassing APS and ignoring the patient''s statements", "Respect the patient''s autonomy and do not file a report, as a competent patient''s refusal overrides the physician''s mandatory reporting duty"]'::jsonb, 1, 'In the majority of US states, healthcare professionals are **mandated reporters** for elder abuse and must file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) upon **reasonable suspicion**, **regardless of the patient''s consent or cognitive capacity**. While the patient''s autonomy must be respected regarding what services they accept from APS (a competent patient can refuse shelter or legal action), the clinician *must* fulfill their legal obligation to report. The clinician should support the patient, document all findings objectively, and discuss safety planning. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK; First Aid Step 2 CK)'),
