    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old female named Clara with advanced dementia is brought to the hospital with a subdural hematoma and multiple facial bruises. The medical team strongly suspects physical abuse by the primary caregiver. Clara has no designated surrogate decision-maker and lacks decision-making capacity. The caregiver demands to discharge the patient home immediately, and the patient repeats, ''I want to go home.'' What is the most appropriate clinical and legal course of action?', '["Confront the caregiver aggressively in the hospital hallway and refuse to let them leave without a written confession", "Allow the discharge but schedule a follow-up home visit by a social worker in 2 weeks to check on the patient", "Have the patient sign a waiver releasing the hospital from all liability and then allow the caregiver to take the patient home", "Place a temporary medical hold, refuse discharge due to immediate safety concerns, file an urgent report with Adult Protective Services (APS), and initiate a legal process to appoint an emergency temporary guardian", "Discharge the patient home with the caregiver immediately to respect the patient''s statement of wanting to go home"]'::jsonb, 3, 'When a patient **lacks decision-making capacity** and there is a strong suspicion of elder abuse or neglect, the clinician **cannot discharge the patient back into the abusive environment**, even if the patient or caregiver demands it. The clinician must: (1) place a temporary medical hold to ensure safety, (2) file an immediate report with APS and law enforcement, and (3) consult social work and legal counsel to initiate the process for an **emergency court-appointed guardian** to make safe discharge decisions. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 94-year-old female named Gertrude with advanced dementia is brought to the hospital with a subdural hematoma and multiple facial bruises. The medical team strongly suspects physical abuse by the primary caregiver. Gertrude has no designated surrogate decision-maker and lacks decision-making capacity. The caregiver demands to discharge the patient home immediately, and the patient repeats, ''I want to go home.'' What is the most appropriate clinical and legal course of action?', '["Place a temporary medical hold, refuse discharge due to immediate safety concerns, file an urgent report with Adult Protective Services (APS), and initiate a legal process to appoint an emergency temporary guardian", "Have the patient sign a waiver releasing the hospital from all liability and then allow the caregiver to take the patient home", "Allow the discharge but schedule a follow-up home visit by a social worker in 2 weeks to check on the patient", "Discharge the patient home with the caregiver immediately to respect the patient''s statement of wanting to go home", "Confront the caregiver aggressively in the hospital hallway and refuse to let them leave without a written confession"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When a patient **lacks decision-making capacity** and there is a strong suspicion of elder abuse or neglect, the clinician **cannot discharge the patient back into the abusive environment**, even if the patient or caregiver demands it. The clinician must: (1) place a temporary medical hold to ensure safety, (2) file an immediate report with APS and law enforcement, and (3) consult social work and legal counsel to initiate the process for an **emergency court-appointed guardian** to make safe discharge decisions. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
