    (v_topic_id, 'A 90-year-old female named Virginia with advanced dementia is brought to the hospital with a subdural hematoma and multiple facial bruises. The medical team strongly suspects physical abuse by the primary caregiver. Virginia has no designated surrogate decision-maker and lacks decision-making capacity. The caregiver demands to discharge the patient home immediately, and the patient repeats, ''I want to go home.'' What is the most appropriate clinical and legal course of action?', '["Confront the caregiver aggressively in the hospital hallway and refuse to let them leave without a written confession", "Allow the discharge but schedule a follow-up home visit by a social worker in 2 weeks to check on the patient", "Discharge the patient home with the caregiver immediately to respect the patient''s statement of wanting to go home", "Place a temporary medical hold, refuse discharge due to immediate safety concerns, file an urgent report with Adult Protective Services (APS), and initiate a legal process to appoint an emergency temporary guardian", "Have the patient sign a waiver releasing the hospital from all liability and then allow the caregiver to take the patient home"]'::jsonb, 3, 'When a patient **lacks decision-making capacity** and there is a strong suspicion of elder abuse or neglect, the clinician **cannot discharge the patient back into the abusive environment**, even if the patient or caregiver demands it. The clinician must: (1) place a temporary medical hold to ensure safety, (2) file an immediate report with APS and law enforcement, and (3) consult social work and legal counsel to initiate the process for an **emergency court-appointed guardian** to make safe discharge decisions. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old female named Louise is a resident of a skilled nursing facility. During a routine physical examination, you note multiple symmetrical bruises on her wrists resembling restraint marks. The nursing home administrator refuses to provide the shift log and tells you to ''mind your own business.'' Which of the following agencies is specifically charged with advocating for residents'' rights and investigating complaints within long-term care facilities?', '["The state medical board; which disciplines physicians but has no jurisdiction over nursing homes or administrative staff", "The Joint Commission; which conducts routine facility surveys but does not investigate individual resident complaints", "The Federal Bureau of Investigation (FBI); which manages all institutional investigations involving Medicare funds", "Adult Protective Services (APS); which only investigates abuse occurring in private, domestic residences", "The Long-Term Care Ombudsman; an independent advocate who investigates complaints and resolves conflicts involving residents of nursing homes and assisted living facilities"]'::jsonb, 4, 'The **Long-Term Care Ombudsman Program** is a federally mandated program in every state. Ombudsmen are independent advocates who work to protect the health, safety, welfare, and rights of residents in nursing homes, assisted living facilities, and other adult care homes. They investigate individual complaints, advocate for systemic changes, and help resolve conflicts between residents and facility administration. Suspected institutional abuse should be reported to both the Ombudsman and the state health department. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Current Diagnosis & Treatment: Geriatrics Ch. 12)'),
