    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named Raymond is brought to the emergency department by a neighbor who suspects neglect. The patient is dehydrated, cachectic, and has an untreated fracture of the wrist that is 6 weeks old. The caregiver is uncooperative, smells of alcohol, and refuses to allow social work to enter the home. The patient''s acute medical issues are stabilized in the ED, but there is no other family or safe discharge option. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Discharge the patient to a local homeless shelter to ensure they are separated from the caregiver", "Admit the patient to the hospital for safety and further evaluation, file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS), and involve social work to identify a safe discharge environment", "Discharge the patient home with a prescription for oral pain medication and instruct the caregiver to follow up with orthopedics", "Notify local law enforcement to hold the patient in protective custody at the local jail until APS can investigate", "Perform a closed reduction of the wrist in the ED and discharge the patient to the neighbor''s home"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When an older adult is in **immediate danger** due to severe neglect or abuse, and there is **no safe discharge environment** (e.g., the caregiver is uncooperative, impaired, or hostile, and there are no other family members), the patient **must be admitted to the hospital for safety**. The hospital admission serves as a protective hold while Adult Protective Services (APS) and social workers investigate the home environment and arrange for a safe discharge plan (such as placement in a skilled nursing facility or establishing emergency guardianship). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
