    (v_topic_id, 'A 72-year-old male named Paul is brought to the emergency department by a neighbor who suspects neglect. The patient is dehydrated, cachectic, and has an untreated fracture of the wrist that is 5 weeks old. The caregiver is uncooperative, smells of alcohol, and refuses to allow social work to enter the home. The patient''s acute medical issues are stabilized in the ED, but there is no other family or safe discharge option. What is the most appropriate next step in management?', '["Admit the patient to the hospital for safety and further evaluation, file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS), and involve social work to identify a safe discharge environment", "Perform a closed reduction of the wrist in the ED and discharge the patient to the neighbor''s home", "Discharge the patient home with a prescription for oral pain medication and instruct the caregiver to follow up with orthopedics", "Notify local law enforcement to hold the patient in protective custody at the local jail until APS can investigate", "Discharge the patient to a local homeless shelter to ensure they are separated from the caregiver"]'::jsonb, 0, 'When an older adult is in **immediate danger** due to severe neglect or abuse, and there is **no safe discharge environment** (e.g., the caregiver is uncooperative, impaired, or hostile, and there are no other family members), the patient **must be admitted to the hospital for safety**. The hospital admission serves as a protective hold while Adult Protective Services (APS) and social workers investigate the home environment and arrange for a safe discharge plan (such as placement in a skilled nursing facility or establishing emergency guardianship). (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 88-year-old male named Walter presents with a deep laceration on the forehead. The caregiver, his niece, is present and states that the patient fell in the bathroom. The clinician suspects elder abuse because of the caregiver''s aggressive behavior and the patient''s submissive posture. What is the most appropriate method for conducting the clinical interview?', '["Interview the patient and caregiver together to ensure both stories align and to avoid creating a conflict", "Ask the caregiver to answer all history questions while the patient is undergoing an X-ray in another department", "Confront the caregiver with your suspicion in front of the patient to see if the patient validates the accusation", "Politely request the caregiver to step out of the room to complete the physical examination, and interview the patient alone using direct, non-judgmental questions about safety and fear at home", "Skip the interview entirely and rely solely on the physical exam findings to make a diagnosis of elder abuse"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Interviewing the patient **alone in a private space** is the gold standard clinical technique when elder abuse is suspected. This prevents the caregiver from intimidating the patient or answering on their behalf. The clinician should ask direct, non-judgmental questions (e.g., ''Are you afraid of anyone at home?'', ''Has anyone touched you without your consent?'', ''Who manages your money?''). Confronting the caregiver in front of the patient is dangerous as it can lead to retaliation against the patient. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Lachs & Pillemer, N Engl J Med 2015; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
