    (v_topic_id, 'A 76-year-old male named Howard presents with a deep laceration on the forehead. The caregiver, his paid caregiver, is present and states that the patient fell in the bathroom. The clinician suspects elder abuse because of the caregiver''s aggressive behavior and the patient''s submissive posture. What is the most appropriate method for conducting the clinical interview?', '["Politely request the caregiver to step out of the room to complete the physical examination, and interview the patient alone using direct, non-judgmental questions about safety and fear at home", "Ask the caregiver to answer all history questions while the patient is undergoing an X-ray in another department", "Interview the patient and caregiver together to ensure both stories align and to avoid creating a conflict", "Confront the caregiver with your suspicion in front of the patient to see if the patient validates the accusation", "Skip the interview entirely and rely solely on the physical exam findings to make a diagnosis of elder abuse"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Interviewing the patient **alone in a private space** is the gold standard clinical technique when elder abuse is suspected. This prevents the caregiver from intimidating the patient or answering on their behalf. The clinician should ask direct, non-judgmental questions (e.g., ''Are you afraid of anyone at home?'', ''Has anyone touched you without your consent?'', ''Who manages your money?''). Confronting the caregiver in front of the patient is dangerous as it can lead to retaliation against the patient. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; Lachs & Pillemer, N Engl J Med 2015; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 74-year-old male named George with moderate Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by his granddaughter. The granddaughter is weeping and reports being overwhelmed, sleeping only 3 hours a night, and feeling like they ''might snap and hurt the patient.'' On examination, George is clean, well-nourished, and has no signs of abuse. What is the most appropriate clinical intervention to prevent elder abuse in this high-risk scenario?', '["Advise the caregiver to place the patient in an unlicenced board-and-care home immediately to save money", "Prescribe a high-dose stimulant for the caregiver to help them stay awake and continue providing 24-hour care", "Acknowledge the caregiver''s distress, refer them to social work to arrange home health aides, adult day care programs, and respite care, and provide resources for caregiver support groups", "Tell the caregiver that caregiver burnout is a normal part of life and they must learn to cope without external resources", "Immediately file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) for child-like behavior and demand the caregiver be replaced"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Caregiver burnout, depression, and substance abuse are the **most significant risk factors** for the development of elder abuse and neglect. In this scenario, there is no evidence of abuse yet, but the risk is extremely high. The clinician should intervene preventatively by validating the caregiver''s feelings and arranging supportive services: **social work referral, home health aides, adult day programs (to give the caregiver daytime breaks), and respite care**. This support can reduce stress and prevent the escalation to neglect or abuse. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
