    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female named Ruth with moderate Alzheimer''s disease is brought to the clinic by her nephew. The nephew is weeping and reports being overwhelmed, sleeping only 3 hours a night, and feeling like they ''might snap and hurt the patient.'' On examination, Ruth is clean, well-nourished, and has no signs of abuse. What is the most appropriate clinical intervention to prevent elder abuse in this high-risk scenario?', '["Prescribe a high-dose stimulant for the caregiver to help them stay awake and continue providing 24-hour care", "Acknowledge the caregiver''s distress, refer them to social work to arrange home health aides, adult day care programs, and respite care, and provide resources for caregiver support groups", "Tell the caregiver that caregiver burnout is a normal part of life and they must learn to cope without external resources", "Immediately file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS) for child-like behavior and demand the caregiver be replaced", "Advise the caregiver to place the patient in an unlicenced board-and-care home immediately to save money"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Caregiver burnout, depression, and substance abuse are the **most significant risk factors** for the development of elder abuse and neglect. In this scenario, there is no evidence of abuse yet, but the risk is extremely high. The clinician should intervene preventatively by validating the caregiver''s feelings and arranging supportive services: **social work referral, home health aides, adult day programs (to give the caregiver daytime breaks), and respite care**. This support can reduce stress and prevent the escalation to neglect or abuse. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14; AGS Geriatrics At Your Fingertips; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 82-year-old male named Richard is brought to the clinic by a social worker. Richard has mild cognitive decline but demonstrates intact decision-making capacity on formal evaluation. His home has been flagged by the city for hoarding, but Richard refuses to allow cleanup crews inside, stating, ''I like my things, and I have the right to live how I want.'' How should the clinician manage this case of self-neglect?', '["Contact law enforcement to have the patient evicted and placed in a locked nursing home facility", "Override the patient''s refusal and order an immediate involuntary psychiatric hold to force a home cleanup", "Unilaterally assign a financial guardian to seize the patient''s assets and pay for home remediation", "Inform the patient that because they refuse cleanup, they can no longer receive medical care at the clinic", "Respect the patient''s autonomy; since he has decision-making capacity, he has the legal right to refuse interventions; document the capacity assessment and offer voluntary harm-reduction services"]'::jsonb, 4, 'A key ethical and legal tenet in elder care is that **a competent adult has the right to self-determination (autonomy)**, which includes the right to engage in eccentric behavior, hoarding, or refuse medical and social interventions, provided they have **decision-making capacity** and their actions do not harm others. If a capacity assessment confirms the patient understands the risks of their living situation and appreciates the consequences of refusing help, the clinician must respect their decision. The clinician should focus on building trust, offering voluntary harm-reduction strategies, and coordinating with APS for periodic welfare checks. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 86-year-old female named Alice is brought to the clinic by a social worker. Alice has mild cognitive decline but demonstrates intact decision-making capacity on formal evaluation. Her home has been flagged by the city for hoarding, but Alice refuses to allow cleanup crews inside, stating, ''I like my things, and I have the right to live how I want.'' How should the clinician manage this case of self-neglect?', '["Respect the patient''s autonomy; since she has decision-making capacity, she has the legal right to refuse interventions; document the capacity assessment and offer voluntary harm-reduction services", "Unilaterally assign a financial guardian to seize the patient''s assets and pay for home remediation", "Inform the patient that because they refuse cleanup, they can no longer receive medical care at the clinic", "Contact law enforcement to have the patient evicted and placed in a locked nursing home facility", "Override the patient''s refusal and order an immediate involuntary psychiatric hold to force a home cleanup"]'::jsonb, 0, 'A key ethical and legal tenet in elder care is that **a competent adult has the right to self-determination (autonomy)**, which includes the right to engage in eccentric behavior, hoarding, or refuse medical and social interventions, provided they have **decision-making capacity** and their actions do not harm others. If a capacity assessment confirms the patient understands the risks of their living situation and appreciates the consequences of refusing help, the clinician must respect their decision. The clinician should focus on building trust, offering voluntary harm-reduction strategies, and coordinating with APS for periodic welfare checks. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
