    (v_topic_id, 'A 84-year-old female named Louise with moderate dementia is accompanied to the clinic by her granddaughter. The patient''s son reveals that the granddaughter was recently designated as the financial Durable Power of Attorney (POA) and has since emptied the patient''s savings account. The patient is confused and did not understand the document she signed. What is the most appropriate action for the clinician to address this financial exploitation?', '["Advise the patient to sign a new Power of Attorney naming the clinic physician as the sole financial executor", "Unilaterally sign a document revoking the caregiver''s Power of Attorney status, as physicians have the power to void legal contracts", "Document the findings objectively, file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS), advise the son to consult an elder law attorney to challenge the POA''s validity, and recommend contacting the bank''s fraud division", "Prescribe a cholinesterase inhibitor to reverse the patient''s dementia so they can legally sue the caregiver", "Confront the caregiver during the visit and demand they return the money to the patient immediately"]'::jsonb, 2, 'When financial exploitation is suspected in a patient with cognitive impairment (such as dementia), the clinician must document the details objectively, file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS), and advise the family to contact an **elder law attorney** to legally challenge the validity of the POA (since a patient with moderate dementia may lack the capacity to execute a legal document). The family should also notify the bank''s fraud division. The clinician cannot unilaterally revoke a legal Power of Attorney. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
    (v_topic_id, 'A 70-year-old female named Edith with moderate dementia is accompanied to the clinic by her daughter. The patient''s son reveals that the daughter was recently designated as the financial Durable Power of Attorney (POA) and has since emptied the patient''s savings account. The patient is confused and did not understand the document she signed. What is the most appropriate action for the clinician to address this financial exploitation?', '["Advise the patient to sign a new Power of Attorney naming the clinic physician as the sole financial executor", "Document the findings objectively, file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS), advise the son to consult an elder law attorney to challenge the POA''s validity, and recommend contacting the bank''s fraud division", "Unilaterally sign a document revoking the caregiver''s Power of Attorney status, as physicians have the power to void legal contracts", "Prescribe a cholinesterase inhibitor to reverse the patient''s dementia so they can legally sue the caregiver", "Confront the caregiver during the visit and demand they return the money to the patient immediately"]'::jsonb, 1, 'When financial exploitation is suspected in a patient with cognitive impairment (such as dementia), the clinician must document the details objectively, file a report with Adult Protective Services (APS), and advise the family to contact an **elder law attorney** to legally challenge the validity of the POA (since a patient with moderate dementia may lack the capacity to execute a legal document). The family should also notify the bank''s fraud division. The clinician cannot unilaterally revoke a legal Power of Attorney. (Ref: Hazzard''s Ch. 14 & Ch. 21; UWorld Step 2 CK)'),
